//! Zig 0.16 build for htscodecs.
//!
//! SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
//!
//! The library itself is the real, unmodified upstream C code.  This script
//! replaces the autotools `configure` + `make` pair: it decides the same
//! feature macros that configure.ac probes, compiles the same translation
//! units that htscodecs/Makefile.am lists, and exposes the result as a Zig
//! module, a library artifact, a translated C header set and a facade.
//!
//! Nothing is downloaded, no system copy is used, and no upstream script is
//! executed.  The only generated files are `config.h` and `version.h`,
//! which upstream's build also generates (the latter from `git describe`,
//! which this fork replaces with the pinned tag).
//!
//! Entry points
//! ------------
//!   zig build              install the library, headers, example,
//!                          pkg-config file and licence records
//!   zig build test         run the behavioural tests for the host target
//!   zig build smoke        run the example and print deterministic output
//!   zig build headers      compile and link two C translation units that
//!                          each include the *installed* aggregate header,
//!                          which is the only way to show the installed
//!                          include tree really works from more than one
//!                          translation unit
//!   zig build headers-run  build that program and run it
//!   zig build check        compile the facade, tests and example for the
//!                          six-target matrix without running anything,
//!                          linked statically
//!   zig build check-shared the same six-target matrix against the shared
//!                          graph: every module position independent and
//!                          each target's shared object really linked
//!                          A target whose OS has no dynamic linker is not
//!                          built there: that one row becomes a named
//!                          failing step carrying the reason.
//!   zig build provenance   licence and provenance integrity gate, which
//!                          needs nothing but the sources
//!   zig build provenance-history
//!                          check that the pinned upstream commit is in
//!                          this clone's history; needs git and a .git
//!
//! Options
//! -------
//!   -Dbz2=true             compile the arithmetic coder's bzip2 external
//!                          method (needs libbz2 development files)
//!   -fsys=htscodecs        link a system libhtscodecs instead of building
//!                          the vendored sources.  This is the real
//!                          spelling: b.systemIntegrationOption is fed by
//!                          the build runner's -fsys=/-fno-sys= flags and
//!                          never appears in the -D option map.
//!   -Dlinkage=static|dynamic
//!                          how the C artifact is linked.  `static`, the
//!                          default, is this SDK's existing policy: a plain
//!                          non-PIC archive at lib/libhtscodecs.a.
//!                          `dynamic` is a real shared library, and it is a
//!                          real delivery rather than a relabelled archive:
//!                          the scalar translation units *and* every
//!                          independently compiled ISA kernel are built
//!                          position independent, the artifact carries
//!                          upstream's libtool ABI number in its SONAME and
//!                          its usual .so / .dylib symlinks, and on Windows
//!                          it carries a generated export definition
//!                          because no htscodecs header has any
//!                          dllexport decoration to inherit.
//!                          A target whose OS has no dynamic linker -- WASI,
//!                          whose `std.Target.DynamicLinker.kind` is `.none`
//!                          -- cannot carry one, so `dynamic` is *refused*
//!                          there rather than answered with a static
//!                          archive or a `.wasm` file nothing can load.
//!   -Druntime=true         resolve the native symbols at run time instead
//!                          of linking them.  Orthogonal to -Dlinkage: the
//!                          C artifact is still compiled and installed in
//!                          both, because in this configuration it is the
//!                          *provider* the loaded dispatch binds to, not
//!                          something the consumer links.  The facade's `c`
//!                          becomes the dispatch generated by
//!                          runtime/graph.zig from this very translation
//!                          step's output, and no native library is linked
//!                          into it.  The consumer then calls
//!                          `htscodecs.load(...)` once and uses the module
//!                          exactly as if it were linked.  There is no
//!                          fallback and no automatic retry.  Rejected with
//!                          `-fsys=htscodecs`, which has no dispatch to
//!                          generate and no header of its own to bind to.
//!   -Dcheck-targets=list   comma-separated subset of the cross matrix
//!
//! The aggregate header, and the two front ends that read it
//! -------------------------------------------------------
//! `zig translate-c` parses C with Aro, which preprocesses as well as
//! parses, so `include/htscodecs_all.h` can be read either way round.  The
//! translation has two routes and `translationRoute` picks between them per
//! target: Aro reads the aggregate for itself, or the target's own
//! `zig cc -E -dD` expands it first and Aro parses what that wrote.  Both
//! routes read this repository's headers with this build's own macros and
//! nothing invented, and only the preprocessor that has to understand the
//! target's C library changes.  Fifteen of the fifty-three published cells --
//! nine NetBSD, five OpenBSD and `x86-windows` -- need the extra front end;
//! the other thirty-eight keep Aro.  `translationRoute` names the header
//! line that fails in each case.
//!
//! Per-ISA 32x16 kernels, and the architecture families they belong to
//! -----------------------------------------------------------------
//! The four ISA kernels are separate `addObject` compilations because each
//! needs its own feature set, and one is added only on the architectures
//! whose `std.Target.Cpu.Arch` its own preprocessor guard would compile.
//! For the Neon kernel that family has two members, `.aarch64` and
//! `.aarch64_be`, and the scalar dispatcher calls the kernel on both.
//! `SimdKernel` and `targetBuildsKernel` say why; a single-member test is
//! what omitted the object from a big-endian AArch64 cell whose dispatcher
//! still referenced all four of its symbols.

const std = @import("std");

/// The build half of the runtime route, owned by the API role.
///
/// `addPublicModule` is the only caller: `createModule` for a
/// runtime-loaded facade's `c`, `createCoreModule` for a linked facade's
/// `rt`.  The signatures are frozen; see runtime/graph.zig.
const graph = @import("runtime/graph.zig");

/// The upstream release this fork pins.  Kept in step with UPSTREAM.md.
const upstream_version = "1.6.7";
const upstream_commit = "b9fc194f772e45bb0a1f44b08cbf8697a1384bae";

/// Scalar translation units of libhtscodecs, mirroring
/// htscodecs/Makefile.am:libhtscodecs_base_src with the header-only entries
/// removed.  The Arm Neon 32x16 kernel and the three x86 kernels are added
/// separately because each needs its own ISA flags.
const scalar_sources = [_][]const u8{
    "pack.c",
    "rle.c",
    "fqzcomp_qual.c",
    "rANS_static.c",
    "rANS_static4x16pr.c",
    "rANS_static32x16pr.c",
    "tokenise_name3.c",
    "arith_dynamic.c",
    "htscodecs.c",
    "utils.c",
};

/// Compile the 32x16 ISA kernels with only their required features, separate
/// from the scalar dispatcher. Target features select Zig's machine code.
///
/// `exports` is what each kernel actually *defines*, read out of that kernel
/// source, and it is deliberately not the header's declaration list.  For the
/// SSE4 kernel the two lists differ by exactly one name: the header declares
/// three of them under "Only the O0 decoder for now"
/// (htscodecs/rANS_static32x16pr.h:81) -- the O0 encoder at `:83`-`:86` and
/// the O0 and O1 decoders at `:88`-`:96` -- while rANS_static32x16pr_sse4.c
/// defines only the two decoders, because its O0 encoder is inside the
/// `#if 0` block at `:113`-`:460`.  A list read off the declarations would
/// therefore name `rans_compress_O0_32x16_sse4`, which no object this build
/// compiles contains, and both the generated Windows export definition and
/// the installed-header proof take their names from the definitions
/// instead.
///
/// `archs` is the *architecture family* a kernel belongs to, and it is a
/// list because one family has two members in Zig 0.16.  Every kernel opens
/// with its own preprocessor guard, and `archs` is this build's copy of that
/// guard written as a target test: `targetBuildsKernel` has to select a
/// kernel exactly when the guard at the top of its source would compile its
/// body.
///
///   * The three x86 kernels open with `#if defined(__x86_64__) &&
///     defined(HAVE_...)` -- rANS_static32x16pr_sse4.c:36-37, _avx2.c:36 and
///     _avx512.c:42 -- and one member matches, `.x86_64`.  There is no
///     big-endian x86 in any of Zig's target lists, so there is no second
///     member to name.
///
///   * The Neon kernel opens with `#if defined(__ARM_NEON) &&
///     defined(__aarch64__)` (rANS_static32x16pr_neon.c:35), and *both*
///     members of that family match: `.aarch64` and `.aarch64_be`.  Zig 0.16
///     gives big-endian AArch64 its own `std.Target.Cpu.Arch` member
///     (`lib/std/Target.zig:1327`-`:1328`), splitting it the way it splits
///     `.thumb`/`.thumbeb` (`:1374`-`:1375`) and `.mips64`/`.mips64el`
///     (`:1352`-`:1353`), and folding the pair back into one member only
///     where endianness makes no difference to what is generated (`:1444`,
///     `:3671`).  Clang defines `__ARM_NEON` and `__aarch64__` for both, so
///     both compile the kernel *and* both compile the scalar dispatcher's
///     Neon half at rANS_static4x16pr.c:1072, which returns the four names in
///     `exports` at `:1112`-`:1113` and `:1135`-`:1136`.  A single-member
///     `.aarch64` test omitted the object from an `aarch64_be-linux` graph
///     whose dispatcher still called it, and that cell then failed at the
///     link naming four undefined symbols.
///
/// `.armeb` and `.thumbeb` are deliberately not members of this family, and
/// neither is any other big-endian alias.  None of them defines
/// `__aarch64__`, so the kernel takes its own `#else`
/// (rANS_static32x16pr_neon.c:1973-`:1975`) and the dispatcher takes the
/// scalar `#else` (rANS_static4x16pr.c:1148), which hard-codes the scalar
/// 32x16 pointers and calls nothing.
const SimdKernel = struct {
    name: []const u8,
    archs: []const std.Target.Cpu.Arch,
    source: []const u8,
    features: []const std.Target.x86.Feature = &.{},
    exports: []const []const u8 = &.{},
};

const simd_kernels = [_]SimdKernel{
    .{
        .name = "sse4",
        .archs = &.{.x86_64},
        .source = "rANS_static32x16pr_sse4.c",
        .features = &.{ .sse4_1, .ssse3, .popcnt },
        .exports = &.{
            "rans_uncompress_O0_32x16_sse4",
            "rans_uncompress_O1_32x16_sse4",
        },
    },
    .{
        .name = "avx2",
        .archs = &.{.x86_64},
        .source = "rANS_static32x16pr_avx2.c",
        .features = &.{ .avx2, .popcnt },
        .exports = &.{
            "rans_compress_O0_32x16_avx2",
            "rans_uncompress_O0_32x16_avx2",
            "rans_compress_O1_32x16_avx2",
            "rans_uncompress_O1_32x16_avx2",
        },
    },
    .{
        .name = "avx512",
        .archs = &.{.x86_64},
        .source = "rANS_static32x16pr_avx512.c",
        .features = &.{ .avx512f, .avx2, .evex512, .popcnt },
        .exports = &.{
            "rans_compress_O0_32x16_avx512",
            "rans_uncompress_O0_32x16_avx512",
            "rans_compress_O1_32x16_avx512",
            "rans_uncompress_O1_32x16_avx512",
        },
    },
    .{
        .name = "neon",
        .archs = &.{ .aarch64, .aarch64_be },
        .source = "rANS_static32x16pr_neon.c",
        .exports = &.{
            "rans_compress_O0_32x16_neon",
            "rans_uncompress_O0_32x16_neon",
            "rans_compress_O1_32x16_neon",
            "rans_uncompress_O1_32x16_neon",
        },
    },
};

/// Whether this target builds `kernel`.
///
/// One predicate and two callers: `addCoreLibrary` adds the object and
/// `addWin32ModuleDefinition` lists the symbols that object exports.  A
/// target's export table and its object closure must not be able to
/// disagree about which kernels exist, so both read this and nothing else.
///
/// It reads `target.result.cpu.arch` and no host state and no triple list, so
/// the answer is the target's own, and it is the same test written out in
/// `SimdKernel.archs`: a kernel's preprocessor guard decides, and this is
/// that decision in the build script's language.
fn targetBuildsKernel(target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget, kernel: SimdKernel) bool {
    for (kernel.archs) |arch| {
        if (target.result.cpu.arch == arch) return true;
    }
    return false;
}

/// Upstream's libtool ABI number, from configure.ac's `-version-info`.
///
/// configure.ac:63-65 pins VERS_CURRENT=3, VERS_REVISION=12, VERS_AGE=1.
/// libtool derives the interface number as current - age, so the shared
/// library upstream installs is libhtscodecs.so.2.0.12 with libhtscodecs.so.2
/// and libhtscodecs.so symlinks, and that is exactly what this fork produces
/// so a consumer's DT_NEEDED / LC_LOAD_DYLIB name agrees with a system
/// libhtscodecs built by autotools.
///
/// This is *not* the release version.  1.6.7 is the package version and
/// lives in build.zig.zon and in `upstream_version`; mixing the two would put
/// "2.0.12" into a field every consumer reads as the release.
const libtool_abi = std.SemanticVersion{ .major = 3 - 1, .minor = 0, .patch = 12 };

/// The C entry points this build always compiles, whatever the target.
///
/// Every name here is declared by a header this package installs and defined
/// by one of `scalar_sources`; the two facts were read out of the headers and
/// the .c files rather than assumed, and the `headers` step re-proves the
/// installed half of the claim by taking the address of most of them from two
/// separate translation units and linking against this artifact.
///
/// It exists for the Windows shared build only.  ELF and Mach-O default to
/// exporting every global symbol, which is what upstream's libtool build does
/// and what this fork therefore keeps: inventing a version script that hides
/// symbols upstream exports would be an ABI narrowing nobody asked for.
/// Windows has no such default -- a DLL exports nothing unless something says
/// otherwise, and no htscodecs header carries dllexport decoration -- so the
/// shared build hands the linker a generated module definition instead of
/// shipping an artifact with an empty export table.
const public_symbols = [_][]const u8{
    "arith_compress",
    "arith_compress_bound",
    "arith_compress_to",
    "arith_uncompress",
    "arith_uncompress_to",
    "fqz_compress",
    "fqz_decompress",
    "fqz_qual_stats",
    "hts_pack",
    "hts_rle_decode",
    "hts_rle_encode",
    "hts_unpack",
    "hts_unpack_meta",
    "htscodecs_tls_alloc",
    "htscodecs_tls_calloc",
    "htscodecs_tls_free",
    "htscodecs_version",
    "rans_compress",
    "rans_compress_4x16",
    "rans_compress_O0_32x16",
    "rans_compress_O1_32x16",
    "rans_compress_bound_4x16",
    "rans_compress_to_4x16",
    "rans_set_cpu",
    "rans_uncompress",
    "rans_uncompress_4x16",
    "rans_uncompress_O0_32x16",
    "rans_uncompress_O1_32x16",
    "rans_uncompress_to_4x16",
    "tok3_decode_names",
    "tok3_encode_names",
};

const c_flags = [_][]const u8{"-std=gnu11"};

/// The installed include tree, in two parts.
///
/// `upstream_headers` are the headers under htscodecs/ that this package
/// installs beside the aggregate.  The list is upstream's own installed
/// set (`Makefile.am` `nobase_include_HEADERS`) plus the two headers
/// whose symbols this build compiles and would otherwise hide:
/// rANS_static32x16pr.h, which declares the per-ISA 32x16 entry points,
/// and utils.h, which declares the exported TLS allocator.  Every entry
/// here is included by include/htscodecs_all.h, so an aggregate that
/// names a header this table does not install fails the `headers` step
/// rather than at some consumer's compile line.
///
/// `fork_headers` are the aggregate and the fork-owned configuration
/// header the aggregate includes by bare name, so they have to sit in
/// the same installed directory as the aggregate itself.
const upstream_headers = [_][]const u8{
    "arith_dynamic.h",
    "fqzcomp_qual.h",
    "htscodecs.h",
    "pack.h",
    "rANS_static.h",
    "rANS_static4x16.h",
    "rANS_static32x16pr.h",
    "rle.h",
    "tokenise_name3.h",
    "utils.h",
    "varint.h",
};

const fork_headers = [_][]const u8{
    "htscodecs_all.h",
    "htscodecs_config.h",
};

/// The records this fork redistributes alongside the code.  Losing any
/// of these changes what the package is, not merely how it builds:
/// a private repository removes no upstream licence obligation.
const required_records = [_][]const u8{
    "LICENSE.md",
    "NOTICE.md",
    "UPSTREAM.md",
    "CHANGELOG.md",
    "README.zig.md",
    "FORK-LICENSE.md",
};

/// The licence and provenance records `zig build install` publishes, under
/// share/licenses/htscodecs/.
///
/// They are installed by the build rather than copied by a packaging layer
/// so that there is exactly one owner and one copy: `provenance` already
/// fails the build when any of `required_records` is missing, and this is
/// the same list reaching the install tree instead of stopping at the gate.
/// htscodecs is BSD-3-Clause upstream except for three public-domain files
/// under a CC0 waiver, and that per-file inventory lives in NOTICE.md, which
/// is why NOTICE.md travels with the licence text rather than beside it.
const installed_licence_records = [_][]const u8{
    "LICENSE.md",
    "NOTICE.md",
    "UPSTREAM.md",
    "FORK-LICENSE.md",
};

/// The documentation `zig build install` publishes under share/doc/htscodecs/.
///
/// README.md is the fork's own README; README.upstream.md is upstream's
/// pristine text, kept byte for byte as a record and deliberately *not*
/// installed as documentation, because it documents the autotools build
/// this fork replaces and would be actively misleading beside it.
const installed_docs = [_][]const u8{
    "README.md",
    "README.zig.md",
    "CHANGELOG.md",
    "FORK-NOTES.md",
};

/// The cross-compilation matrix.  `check` walks this list and builds every
/// entry in it.  Nothing is pre-judged: if a target turns out to need
/// something this machine does not have, that is for the gate to report,
/// not for the build script to guess at in advance.
const matrix = [_]struct {
    name: []const u8,
    query: std.Target.Query,
}{
    .{
        .name = "x86_64-linux-gnu",
        .query = .{ .cpu_arch = .x86_64, .os_tag = .linux, .abi = .gnu },
    },
    .{
        .name = "aarch64-linux-gnu",
        .query = .{ .cpu_arch = .aarch64, .os_tag = .linux, .abi = .gnu },
    },
    .{
        // Note: in Zig 0.16 `Abi.default` maps .macos to .none, so this is
        // the ordinary, normal macOS ABI rather than a libc-less one.  It
        // is in the matrix because it was asked for; whether this machine
        // can satisfy its prerequisites is a gate observation, not a build
        // script assumption.
        .name = "aarch64-macos-none",
        .query = .{ .cpu_arch = .aarch64, .os_tag = .macos, .abi = .none },
    },
    .{
        .name = "x86_64-windows-gnu",
        .query = .{ .cpu_arch = .x86_64, .os_tag = .windows, .abi = .gnu },
    },
    .{
        .name = "riscv64-linux-gnu",
        .query = .{ .cpu_arch = .riscv64, .os_tag = .linux, .abi = .gnu },
    },
    .{
        .name = "wasm32-wasi",
        .query = .{ .cpu_arch = .wasm32, .os_tag = .wasi },
    },
};

/// Targets outside the requested matrix, buildable only when named
/// explicitly with -Dcheck-targets.  They are spelled without an ABI so the
/// toolchain picks its own default; whether this machine can satisfy their
/// prerequisites is a gate observation.
const extra_matrix = [_]struct { name: []const u8, query: std.Target.Query }{
    .{ .name = "aarch64-macos", .query = .{ .cpu_arch = .aarch64, .os_tag = .macos } },
    .{ .name = "x86_64-macos", .query = .{ .cpu_arch = .x86_64, .os_tag = .macos } },
    .{ .name = "aarch64-windows-gnu", .query = .{ .cpu_arch = .aarch64, .os_tag = .windows, .abi = .gnu } },
};

/// Behavioural test files.  `test` runs them for the host target and
/// `check` compiles them, as objects, for every matrix target.  They are
/// the deliberate callers of the public facade: each method below is
/// invoked by a test that asserts something about its result, which is
/// what instantiates its body.  Reflection is deliberately not used
/// instead; see FORK-NOTES.md.
const test_files = [_][]const u8{
    "test/fixture_decode_test.zig",
    "test/roundtrip_test.zig",
    "test/malformed_test.zig",
    "test/facade_contract_test.zig",
};

const example_file = "examples/codec_demo.zig";

pub fn build(b: *std.Build) void {
    const optimize = b.standardOptimizeOption(.{});
    const target = b.standardTargetOptions(.{});
    const bz2 = b.option(
        bool,
        "bz2",
        "Compile the arithmetic coder's bzip2 external method (HAVE_LIBBZ2). " ++
            "Requires libbz2 development files for the target.  Off by default, " ++
            "which matches upstream's behaviour when libbz2 is not found.",
    ) orelse false;
    const linkage = b.option(
        std.builtin.LinkMode,
        "linkage",
        "Link the C artifact as a static archive (the default, and this " ++
            "SDK's existing policy) or as a real shared library.  Ignored " ++
            "under -fsys=htscodecs, where the installed library's linkage " ++
            "is not this build's to choose.",
    ) orelse .static;
    const runtime = b.option(
        bool,
        "runtime",
        "Resolve the native symbols at run time instead of linking them: the " ++
            "facade's `c` becomes the dispatch runtime/graph.zig generates " ++
            "from this very translation step, and no native library is " ++
            "linked into it.  Orthogonal to -Dlinkage; the C artifact is " ++
            "still built and installed in both configurations, because in " ++
            "this one it is the provider the dispatch binds to.",
    ) orelse false;
    const use_system = b.systemIntegrationOption("htscodecs", .{
        .default = false,
    });

    // Refused rather than ignored.  System mode publishes the *installed*
    // library's own headers and links the installed library; there is no
    // native artifact of ours to bind a loaded table to, so quietly dropping
    // the flag would produce a build that claims a runtime route and links.
    if (use_system and runtime) {
        std.debug.panic(
            "-fsys=htscodecs and -Druntime=true cannot both be given: system " ++
                "mode publishes the installed library's own headers and links " ++
                "the installed library, and this build compiles no artifact of " ++
                "its own to generate a dispatch from.  Drop one of them.",
            .{},
        );
    }

    if (use_system) {
        addSystemLibraryMode(b, target, optimize);
        return;
    }

    // The published graph's own target, before any step is created for it,
    // so nothing is half-built on the way to finding out.  This is a
    // configure-time refusal, and it is about the *requested* linkage: the
    // default `-Dlinkage=static` stays a full configuration for every
    // target in the matrix, WASI included, and so does `-Druntime=true`,
    // which is orthogonal to the linkage either way.  It is the shared
    // variant of a target that cannot host one that is refused, not the
    // target itself.
    if (linkage == .dynamic) {
        if (sharedObjectRefusal(b, target)) |reason| @panic(reason);
    }

    const generated = addGeneratedHeaders(b);

    const lib = addCoreLibrary(b, target, optimize, generated, bz2, linkage);
    b.installArtifact(lib);
    // `Step.InstallFile.create` does not attach itself to anything:
    // `b.addInstallFile`/`b.addInstallHeaderFile` only build a step, and the
    // caller has to make the install step depend on it.  Every install step
    // below is therefore bound explicitly.  Binding the headers here also
    // closes a real gap -- they used to be reached only through the
    // two-translation-unit proof, so a plain `zig build install` installed
    // the archive and not the include tree it cannot be used without.
    const header_installs = installHeaders(b);
    for (header_installs) |header| b.getInstallStep().dependOn(&header.step);

    // --- the rest of the package, not just the archive -------------------
    // A shared object with no linker metadata is half a delivery: this
    // writes the pkg-config file whose `Libs.private` names the same
    // -lm/-lbz2 the C graph links, and publishes the licence and
    // provenance records the tree is obliged to carry with the binary.
    const pkgconfig = b.addInstallFile(
        addPkgConfig(b, target, bz2),
        "lib/pkgconfig/htscodecs.pc",
    );
    b.getInstallStep().dependOn(&pkgconfig.step);
    installRecords(b);

    const published = addPublicModule(
        b,
        "htscodecs",
        target,
        optimize,
        lib,
        .vendored,
        bz2,
        runtime,
        .build_cache,
        null,
    );
    const fixtures = addFixturesModule(b, target, optimize);

    // The same facade a second time, with the other `Loader`, and only when
    // that is a real difference.
    //
    // The published `htscodecs` module is the `build_cache` flavour, because
    // that is what a dependent needs: `linkLibrary` records the directory the
    // shared object was emitted into, and every compilation whose graph
    // imports the module -- a dependent's executable among them -- gets an
    // `-rpath` for it. Every test binary and the copy of the example the
    // `smoke` step executes is the same case.
    //
    // `bin/htscodecs-demo` is installed and is the exception: it wants the
    // install tree's `$ORIGIN/../lib` and nothing else, because Zig's own
    // entry names a directory that is gone once the file is installed and is
    // something Nix refuses to package at all. It is compiled from a second,
    // *private* facade for that purpose.
    //
    // The two are separate compilations of one source file against one
    // library, because one binary cannot carry both search paths. Under
    // `-Dlinkage=static`, under `-fsys=htscodecs` and under `-Druntime=true`
    // both flavours produce the same file, and `needsBothLoaders` says so: the
    // facade above is then used for both, and nothing is compiled twice.
    const both_loaders = needsBothLoaders(if (runtime) null else lib, target);
    const install_facade = if (both_loaders)
        addPublicModule(b, null, target, optimize, lib, .vendored, bz2, runtime, .install_tree, &published).module
    else
        published.module;

    // --- tests -----------------------------------------------------------
    const test_step = b.step("test", "Run the behavioural tests");
    for (test_files) |path| {
        const unit = b.addTest(.{
            .name = std.fs.path.stem(path),
            .root_module = testModule(b, path, target, optimize, published.module, fixtures),
        });
        test_step.dependOn(&b.addRunArtifact(unit).step);
    }

    // --- example ---------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // Installed once, from the private install-tree facade, and -- when the two
    // flavours differ -- compiled a second time from the published facade for
    // the `smoke` step to execute. `addDemo` is the one place that describes
    // the program, so the two copies cannot drift apart in anything but the
    // search path.
    const demo = addDemo(b, "htscodecs-demo", target, optimize, install_facade, fixtures);
    b.installArtifact(demo);

    const run_demo = if (both_loaders)
        addDemo(b, "htscodecs-demo-run", target, optimize, published.module, fixtures)
    else
        demo;
    const smoke_run = b.addRunArtifact(run_demo);
    const smoke_step = b.step("smoke", "Run the example and print its deterministic output");
    smoke_step.dependOn(&smoke_run.step);
    if (b.args) |args| smoke_run.addArgs(args);

    // --- installed include tree, from more than one translation unit -----
    addInstalledHeaderProof(b, target, optimize, lib, header_installs);

    // --- cross-target check ----------------------------------------------
    // Declared once and handed to both graphs: a build option may only be
    // declared once, and the static and shared matrices walk it separately.
    // Selecting nothing means the whole default matrix.  Naming targets
    // narrows it to those and additionally enables any opt-in name; a name
    // in neither list is an error rather than a silent no-op, and it is
    // reported here with both lists spelled out.
    const check_targets = b.option(
        []const u8,
        "check-targets",
        b.fmt(
            "Comma-separated subset of the cross matrix to check instead of the " ++
                "whole default matrix.  Default matrix: {s}.  Also accepted: {s}.",
            .{ knownTargetNames(b), knownExtraTargetNames(b) },
        ),
    );
    addCrossCheck(b, optimize, generated, bz2, .static, runtime, check_targets);
    addCrossCheck(b, optimize, generated, bz2, .dynamic, runtime, check_targets);

    // --- source-integrity and history gates -------------------------------
    addProvenanceGate(b);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The linkage a target can carry
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether this target can carry a shared object at all.
///
/// For most OSes that is exactly what `std.Target.DynamicLinker.kind` answers
/// from the OS tag alone (`lib/std/Target.zig:2345-2403`): the `.arch_os` and
/// `.arch_os_abi` families have a dynamic linker and `.none` does not, and
/// this package's own matrix walks `wasm32-wasi`, which is `.none`
/// (`:2385`).  A `.wasm` module there has no dynamic linker and no loader
/// that resolves a shared object's imports at load time, so a "shared"
/// htscodecs for that row is a file nothing can open.
///
/// Windows is the one `.none` that does carry a shared object.  `.windows` is
/// `.none` (`:2380`) because a PE/COFF image has no interpreter path -- its
/// imports are resolved by the loader inside the OS -- not because Windows
/// cannot load a DLL.  The two facts are read separately here rather than
/// collapsed into one predicate, because collapsing them would refuse
/// `-Dlinkage=dynamic` on the `x86_64-windows-gnu` cell of this matrix, which
/// is a row this package is expected to build.
fn targetCarriesSharedObject(target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget) bool {
    if (target.result.os.tag == .windows) return true;
    return std.Target.DynamicLinker.kind(target.result.os.tag) != .none;
}

/// Why `-Dlinkage=dynamic` cannot be delivered for this target, or `null`
/// when it can.
///
/// A refusal and not a substitution, and there is no third answer.  A caller
/// who asked for a shared object either gets one or gets this text naming the
/// target that cannot carry it.  Handing back a static archive, relabelling a
/// `.wasm` module as the shared object, or emitting nothing at all would each
/// answer a different question than the one that was asked.
///
/// The text is deliberately unprefixed and composable, so the configure-time
/// `@panic` in `build` and the failing check row in `addOneCheckTarget` cannot
/// describe the same boundary two different ways.
fn sharedObjectRefusal(b: *std.Build, target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget) ?[]const u8 {
    if (targetCarriesSharedObject(target)) return null;
    return b.fmt(
        "-Dlinkage=dynamic was requested for {s}, whose OS has no dynamic " ++
            "linker (std.Target.DynamicLinker.kind is .none for that OS), so no " ++
            "shared object can be built or loaded for it. This build does not " ++
            "substitute a different artefact for the one that was asked for; " ++
            "build the static delivery for this target instead, which is the " ++
            "default.",
        .{@tagName(target.result.os.tag)},
    );
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Generated headers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Writes the two files upstream's build generates.
///
/// `version.h` upstream derives from `git describe`; a pinned release does
/// not need git at build time, so the tag is baked in.  `config.h` is the
/// autoconf-shaped header every upstream .c includes; it is a one-line
/// shim so that the real policy lives in the checked-in
/// include/htscodecs_config.h and is shared with translate-c.
fn addGeneratedHeaders(b: *std.Build) std.Build.LazyPath {
    const files = b.addWriteFiles();
    _ = files.add("htscodecs_config/config.h",
        \\/* Generated by build.zig.  Do not edit. */
        \\#ifndef HTSCODECS_BUILD_CONFIG_H
        \\#define HTSCODECS_BUILD_CONFIG_H
        \\#include "htscodecs_config.h"
        \\#endif
        \\
    );
    // One buffer, built with b.fmt: the version text is a value, so the
    // file cannot be assembled with a compile-time `++`.
    _ = files.add("htscodecs_config/version.h", b.fmt(
        \\/* Generated by build.zig from the pinned upstream tag.  Do not edit. */
        \\#ifndef HTSCODECS_VERSION_H
        \\#define HTSCODECS_VERSION_H
        \\#define HTSCODECS_VERSION_TEXT "{s}"
        \\#endif
        \\
    , .{upstream_version}));
    // The directory, not one of the files: both generated headers live
    // under htscodecs_config/, and the C sources reach them by the bare
    // names upstream uses.
    return files.getDirectory();
}

fn addCIncludePaths(m: *std.Build.Module, b: *std.Build, generated: std.Build.LazyPath) void {
    // htscodecs/ for the sources' own quoted includes,
    // include/ for htscodecs_config.h,
    // the repository root for the "htscodecs/..." spelling,
    // the generated directory for the "htscodecs_config/version.h" spelling,
    // and the htscodecs_config/ subdirectory itself so a bare `#include
    // "config.h"` resolves.  The SIMD kernels open with that bare include
    // (rANS_static32x16pr_avx2.c:34, rANS_static32x16pr_avx512.c:40) and the
    // headers reach version.h the same way, so the subdirectory is not
    // optional: without it those translation units do not compile.
    m.addIncludePath(b.path("htscodecs"));
    m.addIncludePath(b.path("include"));
    m.addIncludePath(b.path("."));
    m.addIncludePath(generated);
    m.addIncludePath(generated.path(b, "htscodecs_config"));
}

/// Whether this target keeps the maths functions in a library of its own.
///
/// htscodecs calls log() for real: htscodecs/fqzcomp_qual.c:573,
/// htscodecs/rANS_static4x16pr.c:358 and htscodecs/utils.h:212 all do, and
/// upstream's own htscodecs/Makefile.am carries `libhtscodecs_la_LIBADD
/// += -lm` for exactly that reason.  On macOS, Windows and wasi the maths
/// functions are inside libc and there is no separate library to ask for.
///
/// One function for both the C graph and the generated pkg-config file: an
/// archive that links libm while its .pc omits `-lm` fails only at a static
/// consumer's link line, and a .pc that names a libm the target does not
/// have fails only on the platforms this switch leaves out.
fn splitsLibm(os_tag: std.Target.Os.Tag) bool {
    return switch (os_tag) {
        .linux, .freebsd, .netbsd, .openbsd, .dragonfly, .illumos => true,
        else => false,
    };
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The C library
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn addCoreLibrary(
    b: *std.Build,
    target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget,
    optimize: std.builtin.OptimizeMode,
    generated: std.Build.LazyPath,
    bz2: bool,
    linkage: std.builtin.LinkMode,
) *std.Build.Step.Compile {
    // A shared object may only contain position-independent code, and this
    // library does not compile all of its objects itself: the four 32x16 ISA
    // kernels are separate `addObject` compilations with their own pinned
    // feature sets, and Zig's default (`pic = null`) follows the *object*
    // step's own link mode, not the archive's.  Leaving them alone is what
    // produces a .so whose scalar half is PIC and whose AVX512 half is not,
    // which links and then fails to load with a text relocation.  So the one
    // decision is taken once and applied to every module in the graph.
    //
    // For `static` the field stays null, exactly as before: the default
    // artifact is byte-for-byte the non-PIC archive this SDK has always
    // produced, and there is nothing in it that wants relocating.
    const pic: ?bool = if (linkage == .dynamic) true else null;

    const lib = b.addLibrary(.{
        .name = "htscodecs",
        .linkage = linkage,
        // Only a shared object carries an ABI number.  On ELF this is what
        // gives libhtscodecs.so.2.0.12 its SONAME and the two symlinks
        // upstream's libtool build installs; on Darwin the same field drives
        // the .dylib names and the compatibility version.
        .version = if (linkage == .dynamic) libtool_abi else null,
        // Windows has no "export everything" default, and no htscodecs
        // header carries dllexport, so without a module definition the DLL
        // would ship with an empty export table.  The file is generated
        // from `public_symbols` plus the ISA kernels this target actually
        // builds; both lists name definitions, not declarations.
        .win32_module_definition = if (linkage == .dynamic and target.result.ofmt == .coff)
            addWin32ModuleDefinition(b, target)
        else
            null,
        .root_module = b.createModule(.{
            .target = target,
            .optimize = optimize,
            .link_libc = true,
            .pic = pic,
        }),
    });
    addCIncludePaths(lib.root_module, b, generated);
    lib.root_module.addCSourceFiles(.{
        .root = b.path("htscodecs"),
        .files = &scalar_sources,
        .flags = &c_flags,
    });

    // libm is a real dependency, not bookkeeping, and the answer is taken
    // from `splitsLibm` so the C graph and the pkg-config file below cannot
    // disagree about it.
    if (splitsLibm(target.result.os.tag)) {
        lib.root_module.linkSystemLibrary("m", .{});
    }

    if (bz2) {
        lib.root_module.addCMacro("HTSCODECS_WITH_BZ2", "1");
        lib.root_module.linkSystemLibrary("bz2", .{});
    }

    for (simd_kernels) |kernel| {
        if (!targetBuildsKernel(target, kernel)) continue;
        var query = target.query;
        query.cpu_model = .baseline;
        query.cpu_features_add = .empty;
        query.cpu_features_sub = .empty;
        for (kernel.features) |feature| query.cpu_features_add.addFeature(@intFromEnum(feature));
        const object = b.addObject(.{
            .name = b.fmt("htscodecs_{s}", .{kernel.name}),
            .root_module = b.createModule(.{
                .target = b.resolveTargetQuery(query),
                .optimize = optimize,
                .link_libc = true,
                .pic = pic,
            }),
        });
        addCIncludePaths(object.root_module, b, generated);
        object.root_module.addCSourceFiles(.{
            .root = b.path("htscodecs"),
            .files = &.{kernel.source},
            .flags = &c_flags,
        });
        lib.root_module.addObject(object);
    }

    return lib;
}

/// The Win32 module definition (`.def`) for a shared htscodecs.
///
/// Contents are the always-compiled public entry points followed by the ISA
/// kernels this target really builds, filtered by the same
/// `targetBuildsKernel` that selects the kernel objects in `addCoreLibrary`,
/// so the export table and the object closure can never disagree about which
/// kernels exist.
fn addWin32ModuleDefinition(
    b: *std.Build,
    target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget,
) std.Build.LazyPath {
    const gpa = b.allocator;
    var text: std.ArrayList(u8) = .empty;
    text.appendSlice(gpa,
        \\; Generated by build.zig.  Do not edit.
        \\;
        \\; The exported C entry points of libhtscodecs for a Windows shared
        \\; build.  htscodecs decorates nothing: no public header carries
        \\; __declspec(dllexport), so this file is the whole export contract.
        \\; Every name below is a definition in a translation unit this build
        \\; compiles -- the scalar list from `scalar_sources`, the ISA list
        \\; from the kernel selected for this target -- and every one of them
        \\; is declared by a header under include/htscodecs/.  A name the
        \\; archive does not define is a link error here rather than a
        \\; missing export in a DLL somebody ships.
        \\
        \\EXPORTS
        \\
    ) catch @panic("OOM");
    for (public_symbols) |name| {
        text.print(gpa, "    {s}\n", .{name}) catch @panic("OOM");
    }
    for (simd_kernels) |kernel| {
        if (!targetBuildsKernel(target, kernel)) continue;
        for (kernel.exports) |name| {
            text.print(gpa, "    {s}\n", .{name}) catch @panic("OOM");
        }
    }
    return b.addWriteFiles().add("htscodecs.def", text.items);
}

/// The pkg-config file the installed library is described by.
///
/// Two things have to agree here or a consumer fails in a way nothing in
/// this repository can see: `Libs.private` repeats the native graph's real
/// dependencies (-lm always, -lbz2 only for a -Dbz2=true build), and the
/// `Version:` field is the *release* version rather than the libtool ABI
/// number the shared object is named after, because that is the field
/// pkg-config consumers compare.
///
/// `prefix` is `${pcfiledir}/../..`, not the install prefix this build was
/// invoked with. This file lands at `<root>/lib/pkgconfig/htscodecs.pc`, so
/// `${pcfiledir}/../..` *is* `<root>`, resolved by `pkg-config` at the moment
/// the metadata is read. A literal root written into the bytes is the build
/// machine's directory layout, not the installed tree's: the same prefix
/// copied anywhere else -- a Nix store output, a staging directory, the tree
/// a consumer unpacks -- would send every `-I` and `-L` it hands out to a path
/// that no longer holds the library. `exec_prefix`, `libdir` and `includedir`
/// all derive from it, so this one line is the only thing that has to be right.
fn addPkgConfig(
    b: *std.Build,
    target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget,
    bz2: bool,
) std.Build.LazyPath {
    // The same predicate the C graph uses; see splitsLibm.
    const needs_libm = splitsLibm(target.result.os.tag);
    return b.addWriteFiles().add("htscodecs.pc", b.fmt(
        \\prefix=${{pcfiledir}}/../..
        \\exec_prefix=${{prefix}}
        \\libdir=${{exec_prefix}}/lib
        \\includedir=${{prefix}}/include
        \\
        \\Name: htscodecs
        \\Description: CRAM codecs from samtools/htscodecs: rANS, a dynamic arithmetic coder, run-length coding, the name tokeniser and fqzcomp quality coding
        \\Version: {s}
        \\URL: https://github.com/samtools/htscodecs
        \\Cflags: -I${{includedir}}
        \\Libs: -L${{libdir}} -lhtscodecs
        \\Libs.private:{s}{s}
        \\
    , .{
        upstream_version,
        if (needs_libm) " -lm" else "",
        if (bz2) " -lbz2" else "",
    }));
}

/// Publishes the licence, provenance and documentation records.
///
/// A private repository removes no upstream obligation, and an installed
/// shared object is exactly the kind of redistribution that obligation is
/// about, so these travel with the binary rather than only living in the
/// source tree.  README.upstream.md is deliberately not among them: it is
/// kept byte for byte as a provenance record and it documents the autotools
/// build this package replaces.
fn installRecords(b: *std.Build) void {
    // Each step is bound explicitly: `Step.InstallFile.create` does not
    // attach itself to the install step, so a discarded handle installs
    // nothing at all.
    for (installed_licence_records) |name| {
        const install = b.addInstallFileWithDir(
            b.path(name),
            .prefix,
            b.fmt("share/licenses/htscodecs/{s}", .{name}),
        );
        b.getInstallStep().dependOn(&install.step);
    }
    for (installed_docs) |name| {
        const install = b.addInstallFileWithDir(
            b.path(name),
            .prefix,
            b.fmt("share/doc/htscodecs/{s}", .{name}),
        );
        b.getInstallStep().dependOn(&install.step);
    }
}

fn addSystemLibraryMode(
    b: *std.Build,
    target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget,
    optimize: std.builtin.OptimizeMode,
) void {
    // Same public module and ABI contract, but the compiled library comes
    // from the system instead of this repository, and the translated
    // interface is built from the *system's* headers rather than this
    // tree's.  Translating the bundled v1.6.7 headers and linking an
    // older installed library would describe an ABI that may not be the
    // one on disk, so the two are always taken from the same place.
    //
    // No artifact of ours in this mode: an archive with no objects in it
    // carries no code, so one only asks the linker for a library that
    // cannot answer a symbol.  `addPublicModule` links the installed
    // library itself instead.
    //
    // The option does not reach the translation: in this mode the
    // installed library's own headers decide what it was built with, and
    // this fork's -Dbz2 says nothing about that.
    // `runtime` is `false` here by construction, not by omission: build()
    // refuses `-fsys=htscodecs` together with `-Druntime=true`, because this
    // mode compiles no artifact of ours for graph.zig to generate a
    // dispatch from.
    // There is no shared object of ours on any link line here and
    // `needsBothLoaders` answers false: one facade serves the test binary and
    // every dependent.
    const published = addPublicModule(
        b,
        "htscodecs",
        target,
        optimize,
        null,
        .system,
        false,
        false,
        .build_cache,
        null,
    );
    const fixtures = addFixturesModule(b, target, optimize);

    // The interoperability suite is the right check here: an installed
    // libhtscodecs has to decode upstream's own checked-in streams, and
    // that is a claim about the library actually on the system rather
    // than about anything this repository compiled.
    const unit = b.addTest(.{
        .name = "system-interop",
        .root_module = testModule(b, "test/fixture_decode_test.zig", target, optimize, published.module, fixtures),
    });
    const run = b.addRunArtifact(unit);
    b.getInstallStep().dependOn(&run.step);

    const test_step = b.step("test", "Decode upstream's fixtures with the installed libhtscodecs");
    test_step.dependOn(&run.step);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Published module and translated C
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Which headers the translated interface is built from.
const HeaderSource = enum {
    /// include/htscodecs_all.h plus this repository's upstream headers.
    /// Used for the vendored build, where the library and the headers are
    /// the same pinned revision.
    vendored,
    /// include/htscodecs_system.h, which pulls the installed headers off
    /// the system include path.  Used with -fsys=htscodecs, so the
    /// interface describes the library that is actually linked.
    system,
};

/// Which front end reads the aggregate header for one target.
///
/// `zig translate-c` parses C with **Aro**, which preprocesses as well as
/// parses, so the aggregate can be read either way round: Aro can do both
/// jobs, or the target's own C preprocessor (`zig cc -E -dD`) can do the
/// first and Aro the second.  Both routes read this repository's headers,
/// on the same include path and with the same macros; only the preprocessor
/// that has to understand the target's C library changes.  Nothing is
/// stubbed, narrowed, renamed or substituted on either route, and no
/// architecture macro is invented -- the front end in front is the compiler
/// that compiles the C core, so `__MIPSEL__`, `__MIPSEB__`, `_X86_` and
/// `__ARM_NEON` are the ones the target really has.
const TranslationRoute = enum {
    /// Aro preprocesses and parses, from this repository's aggregate.
    aro,
    /// The target's own C preprocessor expands the aggregate first, and Aro
    /// parses what that produced.
    preprocessed,
};

/// Chooses the front end for one target.
///
/// ## What is observed
///
/// `OBSERVED`, from the parent's 53-platform run and not re-run here: with
/// Aro alone, fifteen of the fifty-three published cells failed to translate
/// this repository's aggregate -- nine NetBSD, five OpenBSD and
/// `x86-windows` -- and every one of them was a failure of Aro's own read of
/// that target's C library rather than anything in htscodecs.  The other
/// thirty-eight translated from the aggregate as it stands, so that is the
/// route they keep.
///
/// The three clauses below are the property each cause turns on rather than a
/// list of fifteen triples, so the next architecture of a family below is
/// covered instead of silently left behind.  `utf8proc`'s
/// `translationRoute` and `erfa`'s `aggregateRoute` argue the same point for
/// the same shape of cause, against the same pinned compiler.
///
/// ## NetBSD, every cell: `_Pragma` framing on `__END_DECLS`
///
/// `htscodecs/utils.h:37-39` includes `<string.h>`, `<stdlib.h>` and
/// `<math.h>`, and `include/htscodecs_all.h:113` includes `utils.h`, so
/// every NetBSD translation reaches three libc headers, each of which
/// closes a declaration block with `__END_DECLS`.  On NetBSD that macro is
/// `__END_PUBLIC_DECLS` (`generic-netbsd/sys/cdefs.h:431-432`), and under
/// the `#if __GNUC_PREREQ__(4, 0)` at `:408` that macro's own definition is
/// framed in two `_Pragma` operators: `__BEGIN_PUBLIC_DECLS` is
/// `_Pragma("GCC visibility push(default)") __BEGIN_EXTERN_C` (`:411-412`)
/// and `__END_PUBLIC_DECLS` is `__END_EXTERN_C _Pragma("GCC visibility
/// pop")` (`:413`), with `__BEGIN_EXTERN_C`/`__END_EXTERN_C` being
/// `extern "C" {` and `}` (`:399-400`).  So each of those blocks ends in a
/// preprocessing operator rather than in plain tokens, which is the one
/// thing about this matrix's C libraries that the other families do not do;
/// the `#else` at `:417-424` is the plain-token spelling every other family
/// uses unconditionally.
///
/// Aro's own handling of that operator is not re-derived here.  What this
/// file records is the header line that reaches it and the cells it fails
/// on; the same header, the same operator and the same pinned compiler are
/// the recorded cause in `erfa`'s `aggregateRoute` and `utf8proc`'s
/// `translationRoute`, and their comments carry the Aro-side citations.
/// Reading Aro's own two halves -- `lib/compiler/aro/aro/Preprocessor.zig`
/// `:1626-1654` and `:3602-3630`, and
/// `lib/compiler/aro/aro/Parser.zig:752-768` -- does not by itself say which
/// of them the failure is in, and this file declines to guess.
///
/// One family, one header, one cause, so the condition is the OS tag: the
/// next NetBSD architecture is covered by the same line rather than by
/// another entry nobody remembers to add.
///
/// ## OpenBSD, two different causes, five cells
///
///   * **ILP32, three cells** -- `arm-openbsd`, `powerpc-openbsd` and
///     `x86-openbsd`.  Aro ships its own `<stddef.h>` and reads it ahead of
///     the target's, and that file typedefs `size_t` as `__SIZE_TYPE__`
///     (`lib/compiler/aro/include/stddef.h:8`), while
///     `generic-openbsd/sys/types.h:171-174` typedefs the same name as
///     `__size_t` under a `_SIZE_T_DEFINED_` guard that Aro's header never
///     sets.  On a 32-bit target the two spellings are `unsigned int` and
///     `unsigned long`; on a 64-bit target both are `unsigned long`, the two
///     definitions agree and the cell builds.  That is why the three ILP32
///     cells fail and the five 64-bit ones do not, and why the condition
///     reads `std.Target.ptrBitWidth` (`lib/std/Target.zig:2913`) rather
///     than a member of the arch enum.
///
///   * **MIPS, two cells** -- `mips64-openbsd` and `mips64el-openbsd`.
///     `generic-openbsd/mips64/endian.h:32-41` refuses to define
///     `_BYTE_ORDER` unless `__MIPSEL__` or `__MIPSEB__` is defined and
///     `#error`s otherwise, and Aro's MIPS predefined-macro table gives all
///     four MIPS architectures `__mips__` and `_mips` and nothing else
///     (`lib/compiler/aro/aro/Compilation.zig:635-642`), so neither macro is
///     there and both cells reach that one header.  They are named as two
///     architectures rather than one because Zig gives them two: `.mips64`
///     and `.mips64el` are separate `std.Target.Cpu.Arch` members
///     (`lib/std/Target.zig:1352-1353`).
///
/// ## `x86-windows`, one cell: a `_X86_` that is not there
///
/// `any-windows-any/stdlib.h:717` includes `<malloc.h>`, and
/// `any-windows-any/malloc.h:183-187` derives `_ALLOCA_S_MARKER_SIZE` from
/// `_ARM_`, `_X86_`, `__ia64__`, `__x86_64` or `__aarch64__`.  The inline
/// functions that then name it -- `_MarkAllocaS` at `:191-197`, `_malloca` at
/// `:206-211` and `_FreeAllocaS` at `:219-224` -- do not compile when none of
/// the five is defined, and Aro emits `static __inline` bodies, so a header
/// that names the macro takes the translation down with it.
///
/// Aro's table is what separates the Windows cells, and it is readable: it
/// gives `.x86_64` `__amd64__`, `__amd64`, `__x86_64__` and `__x86_64`
/// (`lib/compiler/aro/aro/Compilation.zig:436-440`), so the `x86_64-windows`
/// cell satisfies the marker through `__x86_64` and keeps Aro; it gives
/// `.aarch64`/`.aarch64_be` `__aarch64__`
/// (`:691-692`), so the `aarch64-windows` cell keeps Aro for the same reason;
/// and it defines `_X86_` nowhere at all, nor `_ARM_` for the 32-bit Arm
/// members (`:684-690`), so on 32-bit x86 none of the five exists and
/// `_ALLOCA_S_MARKER_SIZE` is left undefined.  Clang supplies `_X86_` for
/// the x86 family, which is the whole difference the extra front end makes.
///
/// The clause names `.x86` rather than "32-bit Windows" for two reasons, both
/// about evidence rather than taste: `.x86` is the cell that was observed
/// failing, and 32-bit Arm Windows is not a cell this package can build at
/// all, so widening the clause there would change a route for a target whose
/// failure is not in this header.  If a 32-bit Windows cell ever does reach
/// this aggregate, the same Aro table says it fails the same way and the
/// clause is where that is corrected -- one property, not a second list.
///
/// ## System-library mode is never preprocessed
///
/// `-fsys=htscodecs` reads the *installed* headers through
/// `include/htscodecs_system.h`, not this repository's, and its target is the
/// one this build runs on.  No cell of the matrix reaches that combination,
/// so no failure of that aggregate has been observed, and giving it a
/// different front end would be a change with no observation behind it.
fn translationRoute(target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget, headers: HeaderSource) TranslationRoute {
    if (headers == .system) return .aro;
    return switch (target.result.os.tag) {
        .netbsd => .preprocessed,
        .openbsd => if (std.Target.ptrBitWidth(&target.result) != 64 or
            target.result.cpu.arch == .mips64 or target.result.cpu.arch == .mips64el)
            .preprocessed
        else
            .aro,
        .windows => if (target.result.cpu.arch == .x86) .preprocessed else .aro,
        // Everything else -- GNU libc, musl, FreeBSD, Darwin, WASI -- reads
        // the aggregate through Aro, which is what it did before.
        else => .aro,
    };
}

/// This repository's aggregate, preprocessed for the target being built.
///
/// The extra front end `translationRoute` selects.  What this writes is
/// `include/htscodecs_all.h` with every `#include` resolved, every
/// conditional settled and every macro definition kept, for the target the C
/// core is being compiled for.  Nothing is stubbed, narrowed, renamed or
/// substituted.
///
/// ## Why this preprocessor may run at all
///
/// `b.graph.zig_exe` is the compiler running this build script, so the
/// preprocessor is the same pinned one that compiles the C core, and the
/// `-target` it is given selects the same libc headers that core sees.
///
/// ## The same macros as the compile line
///
/// `c_flags` is the C compile line's own flag list and `HTSCODECS_WITH_BZ2`
/// the one configuration macro the translation carries as well, so both are
/// repeated here rather than restated.  That is not decoration: this
/// package's whole configuration policy is ordinary `#if`s in the
/// checked-in `include/htscodecs_config.h`, which
/// `include/htscodecs_all.h:79` includes before every upstream header, so
/// the two lines have to settle the same conditionals or the archive and
/// the translated interface describe different builds.
///
/// ## Why no `-I`
///
/// Because the aggregate resolves everything itself.  Its own includes are
/// relative to its own directory -- `htscodecs_config.h` at
/// `include/htscodecs_all.h:79` and `../htscodecs/*.h` at `:95-113` -- and
/// every include that reaches the preprocessor is a libc one: `<stdint.h>` and
/// `<stddef.h>` from the aggregate and `utils.h:37-39`'s `<string.h>`,
/// `<stdlib.h>` and `<math.h>`, which the preprocessor finds on the target's
/// own search path.  No installed header includes `config.h` or `version.h` by
/// bare name, so the two headers `addGeneratedHeaders` writes are not on this
/// line and nothing here can bind to a build machine's copy of either.
///
/// The aggregate's one bare include is `varint.h:52`'s `"varint2.h"`, and it
/// is not reached: that line sits in the `#ifdef VARINT2` branch at
/// `varint.h:51-53`, nothing in this package defines `VARINT2`, and the
/// `#else` at `:53` is what the C core and this preprocessor both read.  That
/// is what keeps the line free of a `-I`, and it is a fact about this
/// package's configuration rather than about the header, so it does not carry
/// to the install tree.  `htscodecs/varint2.h` is in the source tree and in
/// neither install table -- upstream's own `nobase_include_HEADERS` omits it
/// (`Makefile.am:37-46`), `upstream_headers` (`:349-361`) lists `varint.h` and
/// not `varint2.h`, `fork_headers` (`:363-366`) lists the two fork headers,
/// and `installHeaders` (`:1672-1687`) installs exactly those two -- while the
/// installed `htscodecs/varint.h` keeps its `#ifdef VARINT2` branch.  A
/// consumer compiling with `-DVARINT2` against the installed prefix has no
/// `varint2.h` beside it to find, and the `headers` step does not report
/// that, because it compiles the installed aggregate under this package's own
/// configuration (`:1702-1704`), where that branch is off.
///
/// ## Why `-dD`
///
/// Without it the expansion carries no macro definitions and `pub const c`
/// loses them.  htscodecs' own headers are largely macros and none of them
/// is *used* by a declaration beside it: `BIG_END` and the `var_size_*`
/// aliases from `varint.h`, `MAGIC`/`likely`/`unlikely` from `utils.h`,
/// `HTSCODECS_VERSION` from `htscodecs.h:46`, and the `HAVE_SSE4_1` family
/// `rANS_static32x16pr.h:82-145` gates its per-ISA declarations on.  A dump
/// of used macros only would take that whole constant set with it.
///
/// ## Why `-MD`
///
/// The aggregate is reached through `#include` from every header it pulls in,
/// and a `-I` directory argument contributes nothing but its own resolved
/// path to a run step's manifest.  `-MD` with `-MF`, through
/// `addPrefixedDepFileOutputArg`, hands the discovered headers to the build
/// system as this step's own inputs, so a later revision of
/// `htscodecs/utils.h` is not served from the run cache with the translation
/// still describing the previous one.
///
/// ## What is deliberately not on this line
///
/// No `-D` of the compiler's own macros.  `_X86_`, `__MIPSEL__`,
/// `__MIPSEB__` and `__ARM_NEON` are what make the three failures above stop
/// happening, and each one is a statement about what the target is; spelling
/// one here would make the aggregate describe a target the C core is not
/// compiled for.
fn preprocessedAggregate(
    b: *std.Build,
    target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget,
    bz2: bool,
) std.Build.LazyPath {
    // `b.graph.zig_exe` is the compiler running this build script, so the
    // preprocessor is the same pinned compiler the C core is compiled by.
    const run = b.addSystemCommand(&[_][]const u8{
        b.graph.zig_exe, "cc", "-E", "-dD", "-x", "c",
    });
    const triple = target.result.zigTriple(b.allocator) catch @panic("htscodecs: unspellable target");
    // The step name carries the triple because one `zig build check` builds
    // six graphs at once and `--summary all` has to tell their extra front
    // ends apart, the same way the matrix names its own steps.
    run.setName(b.fmt("preprocess htscodecs_all.h {s}", .{triple}));
    if (!target.query.isNative()) {
        run.addArg("-target");
        run.addArg(triple);
    }
    for (c_flags) |flag| run.addArg(flag);
    if (bz2) run.addArg("-DHTSCODECS_WITH_BZ2=1");
    // The generated file is this step's output, so the step is cached: a
    // second build with the same inputs neither re-runs the preprocessor nor
    // rewrites the file.
    run.addArg("-o");
    const preprocessed = run.addOutputFileArg("htscodecs-translate.c");
    run.addArg("-MD");
    // Discarded on purpose: what matters is that the run step records this
    // dep file and reads it back, not that a build-script consumer holds the
    // path.
    _ = run.addPrefixedDepFileOutputArg("-MF", "htscodecs-translate.d");
    run.addFileArg(b.path("include/htscodecs_all.h"));
    return preprocessed;
}

/// The published facade, plus the two inputs a second facade for the same
/// target has to reuse: the translation step it declares `c` from, and the one
/// loader core through which both facades publish `LoadSource` and `LoadError`.
/// There is one core per build and not one per facade; two cores would be two
/// sets of those two types.
const Facade = struct {
    module: *std.Build.Module,
    translate: *std.Build.Step.TranslateC,
    rt_core: ?*std.Build.Module,
};

fn addPublicModule(
    b: *std.Build,
    /// The name to publish this facade under, or null for a private one.
    /// `b.addModule` is keyed by name against the whole build, so exactly one
    /// facade per build registers `htscodecs` and a second facade for the same
    /// target takes the private form.
    name: ?[]const u8,
    target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget,
    optimize: std.builtin.OptimizeMode,
    /// The native artifact this graph compiles, or null under
    /// `-fsys=htscodecs`, where there is none and the installed library the
    /// translation reads is linked instead.
    lib: ?*std.Build.Step.Compile,
    headers: HeaderSource,
    bz2: bool,
    runtime: bool,
    loader: Loader,
    shared: ?*const Facade,
) Facade {
    // A second facade for the same target reads the first one's translation
    // step rather than running a second translation of the same header, so the
    // two declare the same things. Only `build` passes `shared`, and only when
    // `needsBothLoaders` says the two loader flavours really differ.
    const translate = if (shared) |previous| previous.translate else step: {
        const translate = b.addTranslateC(.{
            // Which front end reads the aggregate is `translationRoute`'s
            // call, and the two routes differ in exactly one respect: the
            // `.aro` one hands the translator this repository's aggregate to
            // preprocess for itself, and the `.preprocessed` one hands it
            // what `zig cc -E -dD` already wrote for the same target, with
            // the same flags and the same macro.
            .root_source_file = switch (translationRoute(target, headers)) {
                .aro => b.path(switch (headers) {
                    .vendored => "include/htscodecs_all.h",
                    .system => "include/htscodecs_system.h",
                }),
                .preprocessed => preprocessedAggregate(b, target, bz2),
            },
            .target = target,
            .optimize = optimize,
            .link_libc = true,
        });
        switch (headers) {
            .vendored => {
                // The aggregate already uses relative includes; these make the
                // "htscodecs/..." spelling work for anything that reaches for it.
                translate.addIncludePath(b.path("htscodecs"));
                translate.addIncludePath(b.path("include"));
                translate.addIncludePath(b.path("."));
            },
            .system => {
                // The installed `<htscodecs/...>` spelling resolves only on the
                // installed library's own header path, which the selected
                // `htscodecs.pc` records: not this repository's include path
                // and not the compiler's default one.  Same pkg-config answer
                // as the link below, so the declarations and the `-l` agree.
                translate.linkSystemLibrary("htscodecs", .{ .use_pkg_config = .force });
            },
        }

        if (bz2) {
            // The same decision the C compilation gets, so the archive and
            // its translated interface cannot describe different builds.  No
            // installed public header is guarded by HAVE_LIBBZ2 today, so
            // this changes no declaration; it is here so that the answer
            // comes from one place rather than two that may drift.
            translate.defineCMacro("HTSCODECS_WITH_BZ2", "1");
        }
        break :step translate;
    };

    // `name` is what decides the form: one published facade per build, and a
    // private one beside it for the other loader.
    const module = if (name) |published_name|
        b.addModule(published_name, .{
            .root_source_file = b.path("root.zig"),
            .target = target,
            .optimize = optimize,
            .link_libc = true,
        })
    else
        b.createModule(.{
            .root_source_file = b.path("root.zig"),
            .target = target,
            .optimize = optimize,
            .link_libc = true,
        });

    // Two routes, one decision, taken here and nowhere else.
    //
    // Linked (the default): `c` is the translation step's own module and what
    // the facade calls into is linked into it, so every call is a direct call.
    // `root.zig` still needs the loader core for the two public types
    // `LoadSource` and `LoadError`, and it gets exactly one core module.
    //
    // Runtime-loaded: `c` is the dispatch runtime/graph.zig generates from
    // *this* translation step's output, so the table describes the header
    // this build actually selected rather than another configuration's.  The
    // native artifact is NOT linked into the facade: linking it would let the
    // linker resolve the symbols statically and the route would silently
    // become a linked one.  `lib` is still built and installed - in this
    // configuration it is the provider the dispatch binds to at run time,
    // which is why `-lm` and a `-Dbz2=true` `-lbz2` belong to it and to
    // nothing here.
    //
    // The core module is imported in exactly one of the two arms, never both.
    // graph.zig's own `createModule` already makes the single core instance
    // the dispatch uses and re-exports `LoadSource`/`LoadError` from it; a
    // second one beside it would be two sets of types, and a consumer naming
    // one while `load` expects the other is a compile error at the worst
    // possible moment.
    if (runtime) {
        module.addImport("c", graph.createModule(b, translate, target, optimize));
        return .{ .module = module, .translate = translate, .rt_core = null };
    }

    module.addImport("c", translate.createModule());
    const rt_core = if (shared) |previous|
        previous.rt_core.?
    else
        graph.createCoreModule(b, target, optimize);
    module.addImport("rt", rt_core);
    // The facade is the module every program of this build imports -- each
    // test, the copy of the example `smoke` executes, and any downstream
    // consumer of the package -- and every module in a compilation's graph
    // contributes its own rpaths, so the search path is decided here, once,
    // for every program built from it. Which search path that is belongs to
    // `Loader` and not to this line: `linkForLoader` says what each one is,
    // and a facade published to dependents is the `build_cache` one, because
    // a dependent's executable is built out of a build cache too. Only the
    // installed demo is built from an `install_tree` facade, and it is built
    // from a private one.
    //
    // What is linked is `headers`'s decision: a vendored facade links the
    // artifact this graph compiled, a system facade the installed library,
    // through the same pkg-config answer the translation read its headers
    // from.  `preferred_link_mode` stays at its default, since `-Dlinkage` is
    // documented as ignored in this mode and the `.pc`'s `-l` is the answer.
    switch (headers) {
        .vendored => linkForLoader(module, lib.?, target, loader),
        .system => module.linkSystemLibrary("htscodecs", .{ .use_pkg_config = .force }),
    }
    return .{ .module = module, .translate = translate, .rt_core = rt_core };
}

/// The runtime search path an installed program needs so that its loader
/// finds the shared object this same prefix installed beside it.
///
/// `bin/htscodecs-demo` and `lib/libhtscodecs.so` are siblings under one
/// prefix, so `$ORIGIN/../lib` is the ELF spelling and `@loader_path/../lib`
/// the Mach-O one, and both name this install tree and no other.  COFF has
/// neither: `installArtifact` puts `htscodecs.dll` and its import library
/// into `bin/` beside the program, which is where its loader looks first.  A
/// target with no dynamic loader has nothing to name either.
fn installedProgramRPath(target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget) ?[]const u8 {
    return switch (target.result.os.tag) {
        .windows, .wasi, .uefi => null,
        else => if (target.result.os.tag.isDarwin()) "@loader_path/../lib" else "$ORIGIN/../lib",
    };
}

/// Where a program built against this SDK's shared library must find it.
///
/// The two are not two spellings of one answer, and one program cannot carry
/// both: `Module.linkLibrary` records the directory the shared object was emitted
/// into, because `Step.Compile` appends `-rpath <dir>` for every dynamic
/// artifact a compilation links (`lib/std/Build/Step/Compile.zig:1290-1297`,
/// the block reached from `.other_step`).  That directory is the build cache.
/// It does not exist once the file is installed, and Nix refuses an installed
/// file that names its own build directory at all, so an installed program
/// that carried it could never be packaged -- and on ELF the loader would
/// find the library through a directory that is gone rather than through the
/// one that is there.  `Module.addObjectFile` puts the very same file on the
/// link line and records no search path at all, which leaves the relocatable
/// entry `installedProgramRPath` returns as the only one an installed artifact
/// carries.
///
/// Which of the two a facade gets is a property of the *program* that imports
/// it, and a published facade has dependents of both kinds. The entry a module
/// contributes lands on every compilation whose graph imports that module, so
/// the published `htscodecs` module is `build_cache`: a dependent's executable
/// is built out of a build cache and finds the shared object in the one Zig
/// baked in. Only `bin/htscodecs-demo` is built from an `install_tree` facade,
/// and it is built from a private one for that purpose.
const Loader = enum {
    /// A program `zig build install` writes into `bin/`.  It carries
    /// `$ORIGIN/../lib` -- `@loader_path/../lib` on Mach-O -- and nothing else,
    /// so it finds the library this prefix installed beside it and still finds
    /// it after the tree is moved.
    install_tree,
    /// Everything that is not installed, the published facade included: a
    /// dependent's executable, a test binary, and the copy of
    /// `htscodecs-demo` the `smoke` step executes.  It keeps the ordinary
    /// `linkLibrary` call, so Zig bakes in the directory the library really is
    /// in and the loader looks there.  The entry never reaches a store,
    /// because nothing built this way is ever installed.
    build_cache,
};

/// Put `library` on `module`'s link line, with the search path a program of
/// this `Loader` needs and nothing else.
fn linkForLoader(
    module: *std.Build.Module,
    library: *std.Build.Step.Compile,
    target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget,
    loader: Loader,
) void {
    // Windows links through the import library and has no rpath, so the
    // ordinary call is the right one there for both loaders and is kept.
    if (library.linkage != .dynamic or target.result.os.tag == .windows) {
        module.linkLibrary(library);
        return;
    }
    switch (loader) {
        .build_cache => module.linkLibrary(library),
        .install_tree => {
            module.addObjectFile(library.getEmittedBin());
            if (installedProgramRPath(target)) |rpath| module.addRPathSpecial(rpath);
        },
    }
}

/// Whether a target's programs have to be compiled twice to have both loaders.
///
/// A static archive, an import-library link on COFF and a facade that links no
/// library at all all produce the same file for both `Loader` values, so a
/// second facade would be a second copy of a binary that is already right.  A
/// shared library on a platform with a runpath is the one case where they
/// differ, and it is the only case that pays for the second compilation.
fn needsBothLoaders(
    linked: ?*std.Build.Step.Compile,
    target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget,
) bool {
    const library = linked orelse return false;
    return library.linkage == .dynamic and target.result.os.tag != .windows;
}

/// Upstream's test data, embedded so the tests are hermetic.
///
/// Private to this package, and private by construction rather than by
/// promise.  `createModule` is the call that does not put a name in the
/// build graph's module map (`std/Build.zig:915-920`: it "creates a private
/// module, to be used by the current package, but not exposed to other
/// packages depending on this one"), so a dependent cannot ask this package
/// for this module with `dep.module(...)` at all and gets "no module named
/// htscodecs_fixtures" instead of an import that silently compiles and drags
/// upstream's whole fixture corpus into the caller's binary.  `addModule`
/// would have published exactly that, under a name this package would then
/// have had to keep resolving forever.
///
/// Only the tests and the example import it, and they do so by value: the
/// returned `*std.Build.Module` is what reaches their `.imports`, so nothing
/// here depends on the module having a name.
fn addFixturesModule(
    b: *std.Build,
    target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget,
    optimize: std.builtin.OptimizeMode,
) *std.Build.Module {
    return b.createModule(.{
        .root_source_file = b.path("fixtures.zig"),
        .target = target,
        .optimize = optimize,
    });
}

/// The example program, from one source file and one set of options whatever
/// the caller intends it for.
///
/// The installed `htscodecs-demo` and the copy the `smoke` step executes are
/// the same program built twice, against the two `Loader` flavours of the
/// facade; this is the single description of it, so the two copies cannot drift
/// apart in anything but the search path they inherit from the facade.
fn addDemo(
    b: *std.Build,
    name: []const u8,
    target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget,
    optimize: std.builtin.OptimizeMode,
    facade: *std.Build.Module,
    fixtures: *std.Build.Module,
) *std.Build.Step.Compile {
    return b.addExecutable(.{
        .name = name,
        .root_module = b.createModule(.{
            .root_source_file = b.path(example_file),
            .target = target,
            .optimize = optimize,
            .link_libc = true,
            .imports = &.{
                .{ .name = "htscodecs", .module = facade },
                .{ .name = "fixtures", .module = fixtures },
            },
        }),
    });
}

fn testModule(
    b: *std.Build,
    path: []const u8,
    target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget,
    optimize: std.builtin.OptimizeMode,
    published: *std.Build.Module,
    fixtures: *std.Build.Module,
) *std.Build.Module {
    return b.createModule(.{
        .root_source_file = b.path(path),
        .target = target,
        .optimize = optimize,
        .link_libc = true,
        .imports = &.{
            .{ .name = "htscodecs", .module = published },
            .{ .name = "fixtures", .module = fixtures },
        },
    });
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Installed headers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Installs the public include tree and returns the install steps, so the
/// header proof can depend on the headers and nothing else.
///
/// The installed layout mirrors the aggregate header's relative includes:
/// `include/htscodecs/htscodecs_all.h` reaches every upstream header as
/// `../htscodecs/<name>.h` from its own directory, and it reaches the
/// fork-owned configuration header as `htscodecs_config.h` from that same
/// directory.  Both spellings work from the source tree as well, which is
/// why `build.zig` needs no extra -I flags to find them.
fn installHeaders(b: *std.Build) []const *std.Build.Step.InstallFile {
    var steps: std.ArrayList(*std.Build.Step.InstallFile) = .empty;
    for (fork_headers) |name| {
        steps.append(b.allocator, b.addInstallHeaderFile(
            b.path(b.fmt("include/{s}", .{name})),
            b.fmt("htscodecs/{s}", .{name}),
        )) catch @panic("OOM");
    }
    for (upstream_headers) |name| {
        steps.append(b.allocator, b.addInstallHeaderFile(
            b.path(b.fmt("htscodecs/{s}", .{name})),
            b.fmt("htscodecs/{s}", .{name}),
        )) catch @panic("OOM");
    }
    return steps.items;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The installed include tree, from more than one translation unit
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Compiles two C translation units that each include the *installed*
/// aggregate header, links them together, and optionally runs the result.
///
/// What this proves, and what nothing else here can:
///
///   * The installed layout resolves.  `../htscodecs/<name>.h` and the
///     bare `htscodecs_config.h` both have to work from
///     `<prefix>/include/htscodecs/`, which is a different directory
///     arrangement from the source tree the translation runs against.
///   * The header set the package installs is the header set the
///     aggregate needs.  A `#include` naming a header this build does not
///     install is a compile error here, not at a consumer's.
///   * The header is safe to include from more than one translation unit
///     of one program.  Upstream's `fqzcomp_qual.h` declares its flag
///     bits as `static const int` file-scope *definitions*; if anything
///     in the include chain gave them external linkage, both translation
///     units would define them and the link would fail with duplicate
///     symbols.  Only a real two-translation-unit link can show that.
///   * Every symbol named below is actually exported by the archive this
///     build produced, taken from the compiled library rather than from a
///     header on disk.
///
/// The per-ISA 32x16 entry points are referenced under the very macros
/// the installed configuration header derives from the target, so the
/// target-derived ISA decision is exercised rather than assumed.
///
/// The name taken for each ISA is the one that kernel *defines*, which is
/// not always the encoder: the SSE4 kernel builds the O0 and O1 decoders
/// and no encoder (`rans_compress_O0_32x16_sse4` is declared at
/// htscodecs/rANS_static32x16pr.h:83-86, and its only definition sits
/// inside the `#if 0` block at rANS_static32x16pr_sse4.c:113-460, so it is
/// not in this archive), while the AVX2 and AVX512 kernels build both
/// directions.  Taking the address of a declared-but-undefined name would
/// make this step fail to link for exactly the configuration it exists to
/// check, and it is the same reason `simd_kernels`'s `exports` lists are
/// read out of the kernel sources rather than out of the header.
fn addInstalledHeaderProof(
    b: *std.Build,
    target: std.Build.ResolvedTarget,
    optimize: std.builtin.OptimizeMode,
    lib: *std.Build.Step.Compile,
    header_installs: []const *std.Build.Step.InstallFile,
) void {
    const files = b.addWriteFiles();

    const tu_a = files.add("htscodecs_header_tu_a.c",
        \\/* Generated by build.zig.  Do not edit.
        \\ *
        \\ * One translation unit of a C program that includes the
        \\ * *installed* aggregate header.  Written to fail if the
        \\ * installed include tree is incomplete, or if the header can
        \\ * only be used from a single translation unit.
        \\ */
        \\#include <htscodecs/htscodecs_all.h>
        \\#include <stddef.h>
        \\/* No upstream declaration is restated here.  Every name the
        \\   body below takes the address of is declared by the
        \\   *installed* header set this file included, and the archive it
        \\   links supplies the definition.  That is the whole proof: a
        \\   name the installed headers do not declare is a compile error
        \\   here, a name the archive does not export is a link error, and
        \\   a signature restated wrongly here would only add a third,
        \\   meaningless failure mode. */
        \\int htscodecs_header_tu_a(void);
        \\int htscodecs_header_tu_a(void) {
        \\    /* fqzcomp_qual.h: taking the address of an internal-linkage
        \\       constant is a use, so it stays in the object.  If the
        \\       header had given these external linkage, the other
        \\       translation unit's copies would collide at link time. */
        \\    static const int *const gflag = &GFLAG_MULTI_PARAM;
        \\    static const int *const pflag = &PFLAG_HAVE_QMAP;
        \\#if defined(HAVE_SSE4_1) && defined(HAVE_SSSE3) && defined(HAVE_POPCNT)
        \\    /* The decoder, not the encoder: the SSE4 kernel this build
        \\       compiles defines rans_uncompress_O0_32x16_sse4
        \\       (rANS_static32x16pr_sse4.c:462) and no
        \\       rans_compress_O0_32x16_sse4, whose only definition upstream
        \\       wrote is inside the `#if 0` block above it.  A pointer to
        \\       the undefined name would be a link error, not a proof. */
        \\    unsigned char *(*const sse4)(unsigned char *, unsigned int, unsigned char *, unsigned int) =
        \\        rans_uncompress_O0_32x16_sse4;
        \\#else
        \\    unsigned char *(*const sse4)(unsigned char *, unsigned int, unsigned char *, unsigned int) = 0;
        \\#endif
        \\#ifdef HAVE_AVX2
        \\    unsigned char *(*const avx2)(unsigned char *, unsigned int, unsigned char *, unsigned int *) =
        \\        rans_compress_O0_32x16_avx2;
        \\#else
        \\    unsigned char *(*const avx2)(unsigned char *, unsigned int, unsigned char *, unsigned int *) = 0;
        \\#endif
        \\#ifdef HAVE_AVX512
        \\    unsigned char *(*const avx512)(unsigned char *, unsigned int, unsigned char *, unsigned int *) =
        \\        rans_compress_O0_32x16_avx512;
        \\#else
        \\    unsigned char *(*const avx512)(unsigned char *, unsigned int, unsigned char *, unsigned int *) = 0;
        \\#endif
        \\    return *gflag + *pflag +
        \\        (sse4 != 0) + (avx2 != 0) + (avx512 != 0) +
        \\        (htscodecs_version != 0) + (hts_pack != 0) +
        \\        (hts_unpack != 0) + (hts_rle_encode != 0) + (hts_rle_decode != 0) +
        \\        (rans_compress != 0) + (rans_compress_bound_4x16 != 0) +
        \\        (rans_compress_to_4x16 != 0) + (rans_set_cpu != 0) +
        \\        (rans_compress_O0_32x16 != 0) + (arith_compress != 0) +
        \\        (arith_compress_bound != 0) + (tok3_encode_names != 0) +
        \\        (tok3_decode_names != 0) + (htscodecs_tls_alloc != 0) +
        \\        (htscodecs_tls_calloc != 0) + (htscodecs_tls_free != 0);
        \\}
        \\
    );

    const tu_b = files.add("htscodecs_header_tu_b.c",
        \\/* Generated by build.zig.  Do not edit.
        \\ *
        \\ * The second translation unit.  It includes the same installed
        \\ * aggregate and uses the fqzcomp flag constants too, which is
        \\ * the whole point: one definition per translation unit must
        \\ * link, not one per program.
        \\ */
        \\#include <htscodecs/htscodecs_all.h>
        \\int htscodecs_header_tu_a(void);
        \\int htscodecs_header_tu_b(void);
        \\int htscodecs_header_tu_b(void) {
        \\    static const int *const gflag = &GFLAG_HAVE_STAB;
        \\    static const int *const pflag = &PFLAG_HAVE_QTAB;
        \\    /* Types are upstream's, exactly: var_get_u32 returns int and
        \\       fast_log returns double, and clang treats a mismatched
        \\       function-pointer initialisation as an error. */
        \\    int (*const varint)(uint8_t *, const uint8_t *, uint32_t *) = var_get_u32;
        \\    double (*const log2)(double) = fast_log;
        \\    return *gflag + *pflag + (varint != 0) + (log2 != 0) +
        \\        htscodecs_header_tu_a();
        \\}
        \\int main(void) {
        \\    return htscodecs_header_tu_b() == 0;
        \\}
        \\
    );

    const proof = b.addExecutable(.{
        .name = "htscodecs-header-proof",
        .root_module = b.createModule(.{
            .target = target,
            .optimize = optimize,
            .link_libc = true,
        }),
    });
    // Both translation units are generated, so they are handed over as
    // `LazyPath` values rather than as names relative to a root directory:
    // `addCSourceFiles` takes `[]const u8` paths, which cannot express a file
    // this build has not written yet.
    proof.root_module.addCSourceFile(.{ .file = tu_a, .flags = &c_flags });
    proof.root_module.addCSourceFile(.{ .file = tu_b, .flags = &c_flags });
    // The installed include *directory*, and nothing else: if the
    // aggregate resolved from this tree by accident the proof would prove
    // nothing about what this package installs.  The header directory is
    // what makes `<htscodecs/htscodecs_all.h>` resolve, and it is already
    // absolute, so `cwd_relative` is the LazyPath form that carries it.
    proof.root_module.addIncludePath(.{
        .cwd_relative = b.getInstallPath(.header, ""),
    });
    proof.root_module.linkLibrary(lib);

    // Only the headers, not the whole install step: this proof has no
    // business building the example.
    for (header_installs) |install| proof.step.dependOn(&install.step);

    const build_only = b.step("headers",
        \\Compile and link two C translation units that each include the
        \\installed aggregate header.  Nothing is run.
        \\
    );
    build_only.dependOn(&proof.step);

    const run = b.addRunArtifact(proof);
    run.expectExitCode(0);
    const run_step = b.step("headers-run",
        \\Build the two-translation-unit header program and run it.
        \\
    );
    run_step.dependOn(&run.step);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Cross-target check
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn addCrossCheck(
    b: *std.Build,
    optimize: std.builtin.OptimizeMode,
    generated: std.Build.LazyPath,
    bz2: bool,
    linkage: std.builtin.LinkMode,
    runtime: bool,
    selected: ?[]const u8,
) void {
    // Two steps, not one, because the two linkages are two different C
    // graphs and a compile of one says nothing about the other: the shared
    // graph adds PIC to every module, a link step that produces an actual
    // shared object, and on Windows a generated export definition.  Folding
    // them together would let the default `check` silently stop covering the
    // static graph the moment somebody asked for the shared one, and it
    // would double the cost of the ordinary gate.  Both are always
    // registered, whatever -Dlinkage says, so neither is a thing one has to
    // remember to ask for.
    //
    // `selected` is read once by the caller and threaded in: a build option
    // may only be declared once per build, and this function runs twice.
    // The help text names the facade route as well as the linkage, because
    // -Druntime changes what the facade is compiled against and a summary
    // that said only "statically" would describe a graph nobody built.
    const route = if (runtime) "runtime-loaded (generated dispatch, native library not linked)" else "linked (translated header, native library linked)";
    const check_step = switch (linkage) {
        .static => b.step("check", b.fmt(
            \\Compile the C core, the translated interface, every public facade
            \\method, the tests and the example for the cross-target matrix,
            \\linked statically.
            \\Nothing is run: the test binaries are emitted as objects and the
            \\examples are compiled but never executed.
            \\
            \\Facade route: {s}.
            \\
        , .{route})),
        .dynamic => b.step("check-shared", b.fmt(
            \\The same compile-only matrix against the shared graph: every
            \\module position independent, each target's shared object really
            \\linked, and on Windows the generated export definition resolved.
            \\A target whose OS has no dynamic linker is not built there: that
            \\one row becomes a failing step carrying the reason, so the name
            \\stays in the matrix and every other row still compiles.
            \\Nothing is run.
            \\
            \\Facade route: {s}.
            \\
        , .{route})),
    };

    // Selecting nothing means the whole default matrix.  Naming targets
    // narrows the default matrix to those, and additionally enables any
    // name from the opt-in list below.  A name that is in neither list is
    // an error rather than a silent no-op, and each graph raises it
    // separately so one linkage can never quietly check nothing.

    var checked: usize = 0;
    for (matrix) |entry| {
        if (!wanted(entry.name, selected)) continue;
        addOneCheckTarget(b, check_step, entry.name, entry.query, optimize, generated, bz2, linkage, runtime);
        checked += 1;
    }

    if (selected != null) {
        for (extra_matrix) |entry| {
            if (!wanted(entry.name, selected)) continue;
            addOneCheckTarget(b, check_step, entry.name, entry.query, optimize, generated, bz2, linkage, runtime);
            checked += 1;
        }
    }

    if (selected) |list| {
        if (checked == 0) {
            _ = b.addFail(b.fmt(
                "{s}: -Dcheck-targets={s} named no target this build " ++
                    "knows.  Default matrix: {s}.  Also accepted: {s}.",
                .{ check_step.name, list, knownTargetNames(b), knownExtraTargetNames(b) },
            ));
        }
    }
}

/// The target names, joined from the tables above rather than written out
/// twice, so the help text and the failure message can only ever name the
/// targets the check actually walks.
fn knownExtraTargetNames(b: *std.Build) []const u8 {
    var names: [extra_matrix.len][]const u8 = undefined;
    for (&names, extra_matrix) |*slot, entry| slot.* = entry.name;
    return std.mem.join(b.allocator, ", ", &names) catch @panic("OOM");
}

fn knownTargetNames(b: *std.Build) []const u8 {
    var names: [matrix.len][]const u8 = undefined;
    for (&names, matrix) |*slot, entry| slot.* = entry.name;
    return std.mem.join(b.allocator, ", ", &names) catch @panic("OOM");
}

fn wanted(name: []const u8, selected: ?[]const u8) bool {
    const list = selected orelse return true;
    var it = std.mem.splitScalar(u8, list, ',');
    while (it.next()) |raw| {
        if (std.mem.eql(u8, std.mem.trim(u8, raw, " \t"), name)) return true;
    }
    return false;
}

/// Builds one matrix entry.
///
/// The C archive is compiled, the public header is translated, every test
/// file is compiled with `emit_object` so the Zig front end runs over its
/// bodies, and the example is compiled into an executable.  Nothing is
/// executed.
///
/// The tests are the deliberate callers of the facade.  Each public method
/// is invoked by a test that asserts something about the result, and
/// compiling that test for this target is what instantiates the method's
/// body here.  Reflection over the module is deliberately not used as the
/// mechanism: resolving a declaration's type says nothing about whether its
/// body type-checks, and a reflection-only test can only ever pass.
fn addOneCheckTarget(
    b: *std.Build,
    check_step: *std.Build.Step,
    name: []const u8,
    query: std.Target.Query,
    optimize: std.builtin.OptimizeMode,
    generated: std.Build.LazyPath,
    bz2: bool,
    linkage: std.builtin.LinkMode,
    runtime: bool,
) void {
    const target = b.resolveTargetQuery(query);
    // A shared object this target cannot carry is a row that fails, not a
    // row that is dropped: the name stays in the matrix, the step carries
    // the reason, and the other rows are still compiled.  It is a step
    // rather than a `@panic` because this branch is only ever reached for a
    // row nobody named with `-Dtarget=`, and aborting there would take down
    // a native `zig build check` over a target that step never compiles --
    // and would refuse the static matrix, which walks this same row
    // successfully and is a separate step.
    if (linkage == .dynamic) {
        if (sharedObjectRefusal(b, target)) |reason| {
            check_step.dependOn(&b.addFail(b.fmt(
                "htscodecs: {s} row {s}: {s} No artifact was built for this " ++
                    "row, and nothing was substituted for it.",
                .{ check_step.name, name, reason },
            )).step);
            return;
        }
    }

    const lib = addCoreLibrary(b, target, optimize, generated, bz2, linkage);
    // The linkage is part of the name because the matrix is walked once per
    // linkage: two graphs for one target must not claim one another's module
    // or step identity, and a summary that says "htscodecs-demo-aarch64"
    // twice with different artifacts behind it is not a result anybody can
    // read.
    const tag = switch (linkage) {
        .static => "static",
        .dynamic => "shared",
    };
    // Compile-only: nothing in this matrix is run and nothing in it is
    // installed, so the published facade's own flavour is the one that
    // matters for a downstream consumer and a second facade would prove
    // nothing.
    const published = addPublicModule(
        b,
        b.fmt("htscodecs-check-{s}-{s}", .{ tag, name }),
        target,
        optimize,
        lib,
        .vendored,
        bz2,
        runtime,
        .build_cache,
        null,
    ).module;
    const fixtures = addFixturesModule(b, target, optimize);

    for (test_files) |path| {
        const unit = b.addTest(.{
            .name = b.fmt("{s}-{s}-{s}", .{ std.fs.path.stem(path), tag, name }),
            .root_module = b.createModule(.{
                .root_source_file = b.path(path),
                .target = target,
                .optimize = optimize,
                .link_libc = true,
                .imports = &.{
                    .{ .name = "htscodecs", .module = published },
                    .{ .name = "fixtures", .module = fixtures },
                },
            }),
            .emit_object = true,
        });
        check_step.dependOn(&unit.step);
    }

    const demo = b.addExecutable(.{
        .name = b.fmt("htscodecs-demo-{s}-{s}", .{ tag, name }),
        .root_module = b.createModule(.{
            .root_source_file = b.path(example_file),
            .target = target,
            .optimize = optimize,
            .link_libc = true,
            .imports = &.{
                .{ .name = "htscodecs", .module = published },
                .{ .name = "fixtures", .module = fixtures },
            },
        }),
    });
    check_step.dependOn(&demo.step);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Licence and provenance gate
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn addProvenanceGate(b: *std.Build) void {
    const gate = b.step("provenance", "Licence and provenance integrity gate");
    const history = b.step("provenance-history",
        \\Check that the pinned upstream commit is in this clone's history
        \\
    );

    // Half one: the records this fork redistributes are present.  That is a
    // property of the sources alone, so it holds offline, in a tree with no
    // git at all, and it belongs to every ordinary build.
    //
    // The path is resolved against the package build root rather than the
    // process working directory, so the gate examines this tree however the
    // build was invoked.  In Zig 0.16 `std.fs` has no `cwd` and every
    // `Io.Dir` method takes the Io as a parameter, so this is exactly
    // `b.build_root.handle.access(b.graph.io, path, .{})`.
    const io = b.graph.io;
    for (required_records) |path| {
        b.build_root.handle.access(io, path, .{}) catch |err| switch (err) {
            error.FileNotFound => gate.dependOn(&b.addFail(b.fmt(
                "provenance: required file '{s}' is missing.  The BSD licence " ++
                    "text, the upstream provenance record and the aggregate " ++
                    "public header are all part of what this fork " ++
                    "redistributes; do not remove them.",
                .{path},
            )).step),
            else => gate.dependOn(&b.addFail(b.fmt(
                "provenance: cannot read '{s}': {s}.",
                .{ path, @errorName(err) },
            )).step),
        };
    }

    // Half two: the pinned upstream commit must still be reachable in the
    // preserved history, or UPSTREAM.md describes a tree this repository
    // does not have.  That is a property of a clone, not of a source
    // package, so it is a step of its own: a tarball or a vendored copy
    // without .git must still build offline.  Asked for where the history is
    // absent, `git` fails and the step fails -- it never reports a success
    // it did not observe.
    //
    // `-C` takes the build root rather than `.`, for the same reason the
    // file check above does.
    const verify = b.addSystemCommand(&.{
        "git",      "-C", b.build_root.path orelse ".",
        "cat-file", "-e", upstream_commit ++ "^{commit}",
    });
    verify.expectExitCode(0);
    history.dependOn(&verify.step);

    // Only the source-integrity half runs as part of every ordinary build.
    b.getInstallStep().dependOn(gate);
}
